    {
      "index": "adv-volcano",
      "component_type": "advanced_viz",
      "title": "Effect size against significance",
      "workflow_tag": "ampliseq",
      "data_collection_tag": "ancombc_results",
      "wf_id": {
        "$oid": "646b0f3c1e4a2d7f8e5b8ca3"
      },
      "dc_id": {
        "$oid": "646b0f3c1e4a2d7f8e5b8caa"
      },
      "dc_config": {
        "type": null,
        "metatype": null,
        "description": "ANCOM-BC differential abundance at Phylum level (from 5 raw CSV slices)",
        "data_collection_tag": "ancombc_results",
        "dc_specific_properties": null,
        "_id": {
          "$oid": "646b0f3c1e4a2d7f8e5b8caa"
        }
      },
      "cols_json": {},
      "parent_index": null,
      "last_updated": "2026-08-29T00:00:00.000000",
      "description": "Which taxa differ between groups, by how much and how surely? Each point is one taxon in one contrast, with the log fold change on the x axis (right means more abundant than in the reference group) and the significance, as -log10 of the ANCOM-BC q-value, on y. Pink points are up and blue points down when they count as a hit, and the View switch adds an MA and a QQ plot.\nHow to read: pink or blue points in the upper corners are the calls to follow up, and grey points are not significant or change too little. A point high on the plot but near zero on x is significant yet a small change, so read both axes. Pick a contrast in the filter first, then open the results table below for the exact values.\nLines and annotations: the volcano draws dotted vertical lines at log fold change -1 and +1 and a dotted horizontal line at q = 0.05 on the -log10 scale; a point must clear both to be a hit, and the six strongest are labelled. The MA view draws dotted horizontal lines at -1 and +1, and the QQ view a dashed diagonal (the expected spread under no differences), a grey 95% envelope around it and the inflation factor lambda in the corner. The 0.05 is the significance level ampliseq passes to ANCOM-BC, and the effect cut-off of 1 is ampliseq's own default (ancombc_effect_size).\nSources: ANCOM-BC, Lin and Peddada 2020, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-020-17041-7; ampliseq ANCOM-BC settings, https://github.com/nf-core/ampliseq/blob/2.18.0/conf/modules.config; effect size threshold of 1 (ancombc_effect_size) and significance threshold of 0.05 (ancombc_significance), the defaults in the pipeline's https://github.com/nf-core/ampliseq/blob/2.18.0/nextflow_schema.json\nNote: the q-values use QIIME 2's documented default adjustment, Holm, which ampliseq leaves unchanged. ANCOM-BC drops taxa seen in fewer than 10% of the samples and samples under 500 reads before testing, and assumes that fewer than about 25% of the taxa differ between groups.",
      "title_size": "sm",
      "title_align": "left",
      "use": "qiime2/volcano",
      "catalog_source": {
        "toolId": "qiime2",
        "toolName": "QIIME 2",
        "outputId": "qiime2_ancombc",
        "use": "qiime2/volcano",
        "description": "ANCOM-BC differential-abundance results. The tool writes one CSV per statistic per contrast; the recipe joins + melts them into a long table."
      },
      "section": "Volcano and ranked effects",
      "viz_kind": "volcano",
